# 6. Billing Calendar and Recurrence Engine

Build a billing calendar and a reusable service for calculating future billing occurrences.

This is an important part of the backend architecture.

## Required functionality

Users must be able to:

- View upcoming payments.
- View payments for a selected date range.
- Navigate between months.
- See expected charges on a calendar.
- Select a date and inspect the payments expected that day.
- Filter upcoming payments by subscription or category.

## Recurrence engine

Implement billing-date calculations in a dedicated, testable module.

Do not scatter date calculations throughout controllers and frontend components.

The recurrence engine must handle:

- Weekly billing
- Monthly billing
- Quarterly billing
- Annual billing
- Month-end dates
- Leap years
- Billing dates that fall on the 29th, 30th, or 31st
- Date-range boundaries
- Cancelled subscriptions
- Archived subscriptions
- Subscription changes
- Relevant timezone boundaries

For example, if a subscription begins on January 31, determine and document how the billing schedule behaves in February and subsequent months.

Avoid calculating monthly renewals by repeatedly adding 30 days.

Use calendar-aware date arithmetic.

Define whether the original day of the month should be preserved when possible and what should happen in months where that day does not exist.

For example, a January 31 renewal could occur on February's final day and return to the 31st in March, rather than permanently shifting the schedule to the 28th.

Choose a consistent rule and test it.

Use a suitable date library if it meaningfully reduces errors, but do not introduce dependencies without understanding what they do.

## Data model decision

Initially, calculate upcoming billing occurrences from subscription records rather than storing every future occurrence as a permanent database record.

Keep the recurrence engine separate from API controllers so it can be reused by the calendar, dashboard, reminders, and forecasting features.

If later features require persistent billing occurrences, introduce them deliberately rather than duplicating data prematurely.

